{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>But when you refrain from vowing, there shall be no sin in you</b>, because those who offer burnt offerings and sacrifices to Elohim have good reward, as it is written (Psalms 66:15), \"I will offer to You burnt offerings of fatlings, with the smoke of rams; I will prepare bulls with goats, Selah\" — \"I will come into Your house with burnt offerings\" (verse 13). And concerning freewill offerings too it is written (Leviticus 1:4), \"and it shall be accepted for him to make atonement for him,\" and it says, \"a sweet savor to HaShem.\" If so, vows are indeed an impetus to the commandment, as it is written (Psalms 116:18), \"I will pay my vows to HaShem, indeed, before all His people.\" Therefore Scripture says, \"Take heed to yourself regarding your vows,\" for even though they are an impetus to the offerings of HaShem which are for your acceptance, if you vow you may come to sin if you do not fulfill it or if you delay in fulfilling it; but if you do not vow, you will find no sin in the matter at all, for even if you never bring a sacrifice all your days, there shall be no sin in you. If so — \"you shall take heed to what proceeds from your lips,\" as you bring forth the matter from your mouth, \"and you shall do afterward as you have spoken,\" to fulfill all that your spirit was moved to bring forth from your mouth.\n\nAnd in this manner Solomon said (Ecclesiastes 5:3–4), \"When you vow a vow to Elohim, do not delay to pay it, for there is no pleasure in fools…\"; \"It is better that you should not vow, than that you should vow and not pay.\" He says that there is no pleasure in fools — those who think to do a commandment by vowing many vows to serve as an impetus for the commandment they intended to do, yet they did not consider in their heart, with neither knowledge nor understanding, to say, \"Perhaps my means will not suffice to fulfill all these.\" Rather, let a person consider that the willingness he had at the time of his vow will be reckoned to his merit. Therefore he warns further (ibid. verse 5), \"Do not allow your mouth to cause your flesh to sin, and do not say before the messenger that it was an error\" — meaning that you should not multiply vows, and when you fail to bring them, you should not say before the messenger — the harsh angel who will be sent against you — that it was an error, for you thought to bring them and vowed them with a whole heart, but now your means did not suffice to do so. For Elohim will be wrathful at the sound of your vows, and the aforementioned messenger will strike the work of your hands."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {0 "The verse citations within Psalms 66 follow the Hebrew verse numbering; verse 13 in the Hebrew corresponds to verse 15 in some English numbering."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:15:16.179343350Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}